Analysis of ultraviolet and thermal degradations of four common microplastics and evidence of nanoparticle release
Laura M. Hernandez a, Joel Grant a, Parvin Shakeri Fard a, Jeffrey M. Farner a,b,*,
Nathalie Tufenkji a,**
a Department of Chemical Engineering, McGill University, Montreal, Quebec H3A 0C5, Canada
b Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Alberta, Edmonton, Alberta T6G 1H9, Canada
ARTICLE INFO
Keywords: Weathering Ageing Secondary plastics Plastic litter Plastic waste Sustainability
ABSTRACT
Many environmental factors affect the breakdown of plastics in aquatic environments, including exposure to ultraviolet (UV) irradiation and elevated environmental temperatures. More studies are needed to understand how these stressors contribute to plastic degradation, resulting in the release of smaller plastic particles. We studied the impact of environmentally relevant UV and temperature (37 ◦C) weathering of four high-production volume plastics (polystyrene, polypropylene, low-density polyethylene, and high-density polyethylene) suspended in water. Particle release was detected, characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and nanoparticles were quantified by nanoparticle tracking analysis (NTA). Weathering resulted in the release of micro- and nanoparticles that exhibited a plastic signature corresponding to the parent microplastic. Nanoparticle release is broadly correlated with an increase in the carbonyl index of the parent microplastic. Aged microplastics were characterized for physical and chemical changes. The impact of weathering on microplastic surface hardness and polymer oxidation depended on material type and environmental factors. Few to no particles were observed in controls, including controls that contained microplastics at 4 ◦C in dark conditions, highlighting the importance of weathering stimuli in particle release. These results show that plastic degradation needs to consider both the parent microplastic and the smaller particles that are formed.
1. Introduction
Plastics, especially those used for packaging, are materials with long lifespans which are often produced for limited expected use. Polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), and polyethylene (PE) represent the bulk of single-use plastics, (Meides et al., 2021) and their disposal can lead directly (littering) or indirectly (poor process controls) to environmental release (Alimi et al., 2018). Roughly 11% of produced plastic waste was estimated to enter aquatic environments in 2016 (Borrelle et al., 2020). The forecast increase in plastic production and subsequent environmental release is such that reducing pollution levels cannot be achieved by improving waste management streams alone (Borrelle et al., 2020). Additionally, the current environmental load represents an embedded toxicity debt, in which the plastic that is already present will continue to impact the environment as these plastics age, regardless of future inputs (Rillig et al., 2021).
Microplastics (particles >~1000 nm) have been observed worldwide, and more comprehensive data on their occurrence and distribution in natural environments have recently been reported in the literature (Alimi et al., 2018; Nguyen et al., 2019; Barchiesi et al., 2021; Li et al., 2020; Wu et al., 2022; Sarijan et al., 2021). Current sampling methods include beach combing (macroscopic marine debris), biological sampling of wildlife (ingested plastics), investigations of marine sediments, (Coyle et al., 2020) and most commonly, filtration for plastic debris collection from surface water (Nguyen et al., 2019; Wang et al., 2022). These sampling techniques are mainly suitable for detecting microplastics and larger particles, and as a result, there is little information reported on the environmental concentrations of nanoplastics (<~1000 nm) (Gigault et al., 2021; Jakubowicz et al., 2021). The weathering of microplastics can result in structural changes of polymers (e.g., macromolecular group modification, reduced molecular mass due to chain scission, change in crystallinity content, formation of
