astic degradation and nanoparticle formation (Sorasan et al., 2021). This latter work agrees with results in which the combination of UV and mechanical shear led to rapid degradation of PE microplastics (Lapointe et al., 2020). In quiescent conditions without UV light, weathering of polyethylene terephthalate and PP thin films at temperatures up to 36.9 ◦C led to visible pitting of the surfaces in SEM images and the production of micro- and nanoparticles after 620 days (Qin et al., 2022). The addition of a Fenton-like reaction to produce reactive oxygen species led to greater degradation and particle release. Considerable uncertainty remains regarding the relative impacts of weathering processes across plastic types (e.g., UV versus thermal, PS versus PP). Furthermore, reports generally focus on bulk plastic or microplastics, while few studies aimed to characterize the release of smaller particles as a result of plastic weathering (Alimi et al., 2021). This study examines the release of nanoparticles resulting from the breakdown of four commonly used polymers under two weathering scenarios. Degradation was achieved via laboratory weathering experiments to expose suspended microplastics of PS, PP, HDPE, and low-density polyethylene (LDPE) to either UV irradiation or elevated temperature (37 ◦C), to understand the relative influence of these environmental factors.
2. Materials and methods
2.1. Weathering microplastics with UV irradiation or thermal stimulus
Plastic sheets of PS, PP, HDPE, and LDPE were ordered from McMaster Carr (see details in SI). While none of the polymers had added dyes, the makeup and specific composition of any additives in the PP, HDPE, and LDPE are unknown. Plastic sheets were cut with clean stainless-steel shears into large microplastics (<5 mm) with dimensions 4.3 mm × 4.3 mm × 1.6 mm (herein termed “prepared microplastics”). All glassware was cleaned with three rinses of RO water followed by three rinses of acetone. For each plastic-type, six clean borosilicate glass test tubes were filled with 8 mL of filtered RO water. Five prepared microplastics of the same plastic-type were placed in each test tube (Fig. S1) which were
covered with borosilicate glass plates to reduce contamination from atmospheric deposition. Additionally, procedural blanks containing only RO water were prepared and placed in the same conditions as the samples to account for any contamination arising from sample preparation or processing. UV irradiation and high temperature conditions were tested individually to determine their relative impact on the weathering of plastics. For UV treatment, three samples and one procedural blank for each of the 4 plastics were placed into a UV-light chamber. The total UV radiant
exposure was 2.50 × 108 J m-2 (see SI for details and calculations). For high temperature treatment (T), the same number of samples and blanks were placed in a dark incubator (Labnet, 311 DS Digital Shaking Incubator) set to 37 ◦C. Exposures were maintained for 18 weeks after which the prepared microplastics were removed and placed in a desiccator until further analysis. The remaining liquid from each test tube (hereafter termed UV-leachate, T-leachate, UV-procedural blank, and T-procedural blank) was characterized for the presence of small microparticles and nanoparticles. Three control samples (5 prepared microplastics in RO water) of each plastic type were placed in a refrigerator (4 ◦C) and covered with aluminum foil, herein termed fridge-control. Additionally, a procedural blank was prepared and placed at 4 ◦C and with no light (termed fridge control-procedural blank). Leachates and prepared microplastics were analyzed after 18 weeks of fridge treatment.
2.2. Analysis of particles in leachates using SEM and NTA
Leachates, controls, and blanks were analyzed in triplicate for the presence of released micro-sized and nano-sized particles by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and nanoparticle tracking analysis (NTA). Details on sample preparation, SEM analysis, and image analysis are in the SI. The number of nanoparticles < ~1 μm was determined using nanoparticle tracking analysis (NTA, LM14 instrument with 532 nm green laser, NanoSight Ltd.) by introducing 1 mL of leachate into the NTA chamber. A 60 s video was taken (335 shutter aperture, 0–5 gain) and over 200 particles/run were tracked with the NanoSight software. Runs were repeated three times per triplicate (9 times for each leachate), between which the chamber was cleaned with acetone and water. A fresh sample was used for each run.
2.3. Characterization of the chemical composition of leachates and prepared microplastics
Approximately 3 mL of leachate for each plastic and treatment was drop-cast onto aluminum foil. A droplet was added to the aluminum foil, the droplet was left to dry, and this process was repeated approximately 40 times until an observable film developed on the aluminum foil (Fig. S2). Pristine prepared microplastics, weathered prepared microplastics (UV and temperature), and dried films were characterized using a Spectrum TWO Fourier Transform Infrared Spectrometer (FTIR) instrument with a single-bounce diamond (PerkinElmer) in attenuated total reflection mode (ATR) (see parameters in SI). Each sample was analyzed in triplicate.
2.4. Vickers microhardness of pristine and weathered prepared microplastics
Microhardness measurements (Clark CM100 AT Vickers microhardness instrument) were collected by indenting the surface of each pristine, weathered, and fridge-control prepared microplastic using a pyramid-shaped diamond head (10 gf over 10 s, see details in SI). Ten indentations were conducted for pristine plastic, which was reduced to 5 indentations for weathered plastics due to resource constraints. Reducing the number of measurements was not observed to impact the magnitude of variance in the data.
