3. Results and discussion
3.1. Size and number of particles in the leachates
Fig. 1 shows SEM images of dried leachates taken at both low and high magnifications, which span a wide range to better characterize the particles present in the leachate (see SI for details). The particle sizes observed at these two magnifications overlapped and are thus presented separately to avoid conflation. The results of particle sizing measurements (using ImageJ) show that all the leachates were mainly composed of nanoparticles (Table S1). The particles appeared to have irregular morphologies and a high degree of polydispersity. Size distributions of particles observed in the leachates are shown in Fig. 1, inset and Fig. S3. At both magnifications, the polydispersity of released particle sizes was high. However, the average diameters confirm that observed particles were mainly in the < 1 μm range (Table S1). As such, the number concentration of particles can be approximated using NTA (Fig. 2). NTA was used to provide an order of magnitude estimate of the particle number concentration for nanoparticles. From these data (Fig. 2), all plastic leachates were observed to contain approximately
