Thus, we conclude that each of the plastic types, upon being exposed to UV or thermal treatment for 18 weeks, released plastic particles into the leachate. Drop-casting ~3 mL supernatant from the fridge-control (with prepared microplastics) and procedural blanks (no microplastics) yielded no thin layer for analysis, further confirming that there was no substantial particulate contamination in this study. In general, the absorbance intensity was low in FTIR spectra obtained from drop-cast leachates. Thus, no attempt to analyze the extent of weathering was made using these samples. Differences in the FTIR spectra between weathered and pristine samples were observed from FTIR measurements performed using prepared microplastics. The formation of carbonyl groups (-C=O,
1550–1815 cm-1) in prepared microplastics confirms oxidation of the weathered surface (Vilaplana et al., 2006). The carbonyl index (CI, Fig. 4, see SI for details) presents a relative measure of oxidation with which to compare the various treatments. The CI increased for PS, HDPE, and LDPE microplastics following both UV- and T-weathering (p-value < 0.1). Only LDPE samples yielded a statistical difference in CI between UV and temperature weathering (p = 0.047). No change in CI was observed for PP following either UV- or T-weathering.
3.3. Microhardness changes in prepared microplastics after weathering
Fig. S6 shows the microhardness values for the four different plastics in pristine condition (before weathering), after 18 weeks of either UVirradiation or high temperature, and after 18 weeks of fridge treatment. The microhardness of the pristine samples decreased as follows: PS > PP > HDPE > LDPE. This can be directly correlated to the different chemical structures of the polyolefins. For instance, PS, bearing phenyl
groups, is much more rigid (glass transition temperature Tg,PS ~ 100 oC) than LDPE, consisting solely of branched and aliphatic chains (Tg,LDPE
typically < 100 oC) (Greene, 2021). A marked increase in microhardness from ~13.5–16.0 Hv was measured at the surface of PS after being irradiated with UV for 18 weeks. This result may be explained by a higher degree of crystallinity of UV-exposed PS, as recently reported by Castro Monsores et al. (2021) wherein polystyrene plates exhibited both higher microhardness and higher degrees of crystallinity after being exposed to UV-B (280–320 nm) for 14 days or more. This latter structural change was attributed to crystalline rearrangement after cleavage of the chains. Similar impacts of UV exposure were observed for PS over short time frames by Meides et al., who also observed an increase in elongation at break for PS tensile bars. This was attributed to a temporary increase in polymer degree of crosslinking, although extensibility decreased as
weathering continued (Meides et al., 2021). Polypropylene and LDPE microplastics exhibited a decrease in microhardness after being exposed to 37 ◦C for 18 weeks. Thermolysis and thermo-oxidative degradation at the surface of the plastics are likely causes of LDPE softening. For PP, the CI did not change after weathering, suggesting no evidence of surface oxidation. The decrease in microhardness in PP samples following thermal weathering can then be primarily attributed to thermolysis (random scission followed by radical transfer process), (Saikrasun and Saengsuwan, 2009) which cannot be easily observed by FTIR. As temperature increases, the formation of radicals is accelerated, and the mobility of the chains increases, which favors depolymerization (McKeen, 2014). In principle, we would expect HDPE to also exhibit a decrease in microhardness following exposure to high temperature. However, in this experiment, the results were not significantly different possibly because of heterogeneity within samples or the precision of the instrument. While the prepared microplastics were floating in RO water during treatment, no visual degradation of the samples by hydrolysis or swelling was observed throughout the study. This is not surprising; all four microplastics are polyolefins which are strongly hydrophobic and particularly resistant to hydrolysis. Thus the timescale for degradation due solely to water exposure is expected to be much longer than examined here. A change in coloration was observed for polystyrene after 18 weeks of UV-irradiation. Although both temperature and UV play significant roles in plastic degradation, it is not straightforward to establish which of the weathering treatments resulted in greater microplastic degradation. The extent of nanoparticle release appears to be broadly correlated with an increase in the carbonyl index of the original microplastic following weathering (Fig. 4). High temperature weathering produced more LDPE and PS particles when compared to UV-irradiation, while also corresponding to the significant formation of carbonyl groups. In contrast, UV-irradiation produced more HDPE particles when compared to temperature. However, particle release and surface oxidation do not necessarily correspond to the measured surface hardness, as neither UVirradiated nor high temperature weathered HDPE exhibited any significant difference when compared to the pristine prepared microplastic. Similarly, the microhardness of PS increased significantly when exposed to UV-irradiation but had no significant change when exposed to high temperature. While microhardness is directly related to the bulk molecular structure of the polymers, particle formation is related to polymer chain breakage and the generation of micro-cracks (Meides et al., 2021). HDPE exhibits a higher melting point (~ 125 ◦C) than LDPE (~ 110 ◦C) and is characterized by a higher heat resistance, thus, it should be less susceptible to thermal decomposition. In our study, LDPE produced more particles after temperature weathering than HDPE (Fig. 2, circles). No difference was observed in the CI following temperature weathering between HDPE and LDPE (α = 0.05), further suggesting the influence of thermal, rather than oxidative, processes. Conversely, HDPE released more particles and exhibited a greater increase in CI (Fig. 4) than LDPE following UV weathering (α = 0.05). Due mainly to the lack of side-chain-branching, HDPE is considered to have greater UV resistance as a bulk characteristic compared to LDPE. This sturdiness of bulk HDPE is observed in the lack of significant change in surface hardness following either UV or temperature weathering, whereas significant differences were observed for LDPE (α = 0.05) (Fig. S6). Other studies, despite different experimental designs, report similar degradation of microplastics that agree with our findings. Biber et al. (2019) observed that degradation, measured as carbonyl content by FTIR, was greater for PS than PE. Furthermore, Meides et al. (2021) observed a multi-stage degradation that initially proceeded slowly as photooxidation occurred in a near-surface layer. Eventually, the formation of micro-cracks and changes to the bulk structure led to an increase in the degradation rate, measured as changes in the average microparticle size (Meides et al., 2021). They observed a decrease in the
