4. Conclusion
We examined the impact of environmentally relevant UV exposure and temperature ageing on large microplastics of PS, PP, LDPE, and HDPE and demonstrate the release of micro- and nanoparticles. This study shows that all four tested plastics release nanoparticles, although differences were observed in the physical (surface hardness) and chemical (CI, FTIR) responses. Plastic degradation should be considered a broad term, as the result of weathering will depend on the considered endpoint (e.g., particle formation, changes in bulk properties), plastic type (e.g., PS, PP, PE), and weathering mechanism (e.g., UV, T). Technology to simultaneously observe and characterize nanoplastics is rapidly being developed, which will allow a more in-depth understanding of the formation dynamics of these small particles. Despite current limitations in detection, nanoplastics already exist in the environment, where their bioavailability and both short- and long-term effects on organisms remain poorly defined. Future research should focus on detailing the mechanism by which these micro- and nanoparticles are released. For example, it remains unclear why changes in CI are the same for UV irradiation and thermal weathering, except for LDPE, in this work. It would be beneficial to determine the molecular response of each plastic to environmental stressors, including but not limited to, UV irradiation and extreme temperatures. Furthermore, while the presence of additives was not observed in this work, we cannot rule out their influence on the plastic responses to weathering in this study, except for PS (100% acrylic copolymer). Future work to detect and quantify additive leaching following weathering may improve our understanding.
